  {
   "id": 3,
   "ref": "110–114",
   "en": "But I am adrift, uncertain about the fates, not knowing whether Jupiter wants one single city for both Tyrians and the exiles from Troy, or would approve of the two peoples mingling, or of treaties joining them. You are his wife; it is right for you to sound out his mind by pleading. Lead on; I will follow.'",
   "en_marked": "[But|1] [I am|4] [adrift|4], [uncertain|3] [about the fates|2], {not knowing|Added to make the English flow: the Latin moves straight from incerta (uncertain) to the question si (whether).} [whether|5] [Jupiter|6] [wants|9] [one single|7] [city|11] for both [Tyrians|10] [and|12] the [exiles|13] [from Troy|12], [or|14] [would approve of|15] the two [peoples|16] [mingling|14], [or|17] of [treaties|18] [joining|19] them. [You|20] {are his|Added: the Latin has no verb here (tu coniunx, you his wife), so the translator supplies one.} [wife|21]; it is [right|23] [for you|22] [to sound out|25] his [mind|24] [by pleading|26]. [Lead on|27]; [I will follow|28].'",
