 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np8-01",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "quin potius pacem aeternam pactosque hymenaeos exercemus?",
   "trans": "why don't we work instead for lasting peace and an agreed marriage?",
   "line": "99–100",
   "body": "Juno frames her offer as a question, which makes it sound friendly and reasonable rather than a demand.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-02",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Alliteration",
   "quote": "pacem … pactosque",
   "trans": "peace … agreed",
   "line": "99",
   "body": "The repeated p sounds link peace with the marriage pact, and the near-pun on pax and pactus makes the deal sound neat and settled.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "pacem aeternam",
   "trans": "lasting peace",
   "line": "99",
   "body": "Readers know that Carthage and Rome will fight three wars. Juno's \"eternal peace\" is bitterly ironic.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "tota quod mente petisti",
   "trans": "what you sought with your whole mind",
   "line": "100",
   "body": "Juno flatters Venus by telling her she has already won, so agreeing seems the obvious next step.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Fire imagery",
   "quote": "ardet amans Dido",
   "trans": "Dido is burning with love",
   "line": "101",
   "body": "The fire image returns: Dido \"burns\", which links back to uritur in line 68.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-06",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Metaphor",
   "quote": "traxitque per ossa furorem",
   "trans": "and has drawn madness through her bones",
   "line": "101",
   "body": "Love is pictured as a poison or fever that has soaked right through to her bones: it cannot now be cured.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-07",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Balance",
   "quote": "communem hunc ergo populum paribusque regamus auspiciis",
   "trans": "let us rule this people in common, with equal auspices",
   "line": "102–103",
   "body": "Juno offers equal shares, but the people would be ruled from Carthage, which is her real aim.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "liceat Phrygio servire marito",
   "trans": "let her be allowed to be a slave to a Phrygian husband",
   "line": "103",
   "body": "servire, \"to be a slave\", is a harsh word for a queen's marriage, and Phrygio is slightly scornful. Juno's contempt for both of them shows through.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-09",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "dotalesque tuae Tyrios permittere dextrae",
   "trans": "and hand over her Tyrians to your power as a dowry",
   "line": "104",
   "body": "Dido's whole people are to be handed over like property, a reminder that the gods treat mortals as bargaining pieces.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-10",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Parenthesis",
   "quote": "sensit enim simulata mente locutam",
   "trans": "for she realised that Juno had spoken with a false mind",
   "line": "105",
   "body": "Virgil tells the reader directly that Juno is lying, so we watch the rest of the exchange knowing that both goddesses are playing a game.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-11",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Purpose",
   "quote": "quo regnum Italiae Libycas averteret oras",
   "trans": "so that she could turn the kingdom of Italy aside to the shores of Libya",
   "line": "106",
   "body": "The aside states Juno's real purpose: to divert Rome's destined empire to Carthage.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-12",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "quis talia demens abnuat",
   "trans": "who would be mad enough to refuse such terms?",
   "line": "107–108",
   "body": "Venus answers question with question. Her courteous tone hides the fact that she is not really agreeing to anything.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-13",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Condition",
   "quote": "si modo quod memoras factum fortuna sequatur",
   "trans": "if only fortune would follow what you describe",
   "line": "109",
   "body": "si modo, \"if only\", is Venus' escape clause: she agrees in principle, but only if fate allows it, which she knows it will not.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-14",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Fate",
   "quote": "sed fatis incerta feror",
   "trans": "but I am carried along, uncertain because of the fates",
   "line": "110",
   "body": "Venus pretends not to know Jupiter's will, though she heard it in Book 1. Her uncertainty is a polite fiction.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-15",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "unam esse velit … miscerive probet populos aut foedera iungi",
   "trans": "wants there to be one city … approves the mixing of peoples or the joining of treaties",
   "line": "110–112",
   "body": "Three ways of uniting the peoples, each more formal than the last, show Venus listing the possibilities without accepting any of them.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-16",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "tu coniunx",
   "trans": "you are his wife",
   "line": "113",
   "body": "Venus puts the responsibility back on Juno: as Jupiter's wife, Juno can ask him herself. The short phrase is pointed.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-17",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Asyndeton",
   "quote": "perge, sequar",
   "trans": "go on, I will follow",
   "line": "114",
   "body": "Two short verbs with no connecting word end the speech briskly, and the half-line leaves the moment hanging.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Hymenaeus",
   "quote": "hymenaeos",
   "trans": "marriage",
   "line": "99",
   "body": "Hymen or Hymenaeus was the god of weddings, and his name was used for the wedding song and for marriage itself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np8-19",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Phrygians and Tyrians",
   "quote": "Phrygio … Tyrios",
   "trans": "Phrygian … Tyrians",
   "line": "103–104",
   "body": "The Trojans are called Phrygians, after the region of Asia Minor around Troy; Dido's people are Tyrians, from Tyre in Phoenicia, where she came from.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 8,
 "questions": [
  "Juno's offer (99–104) sounds generous. What does she really hope to gain, and how does Virgil let the reader know?",
  "Venus replies politely but uncertainly (107–114). How does she manage to agree without committing herself?",
  "Both goddesses talk about Dido as if she were a piece in a game. How does this affect your sympathy for her?"
 ]
}